    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, faithful instance of the patterns already established in this pipeline \u2014 it is a drop-in sibling of the existing field-validation steps, not a new shape.\n\n- **Right unit, consistent with siblings.** `ValidateRegistrationDate.service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request)` mirrors `ValidatePostcode` exactly: reads the body published by `ValidateNewOwner` (correctly avoiding a second `@RequestBody` bind), does one focused check, and throws a checked escalation. Method name `service`, the `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` signature, and the \"no-op when absent\" behavior all match the existing validation functions.\n- **Exception + handler follow the codebase idiom.** `FutureRegistrationDateException extends Exception` with a message constructor matches `InvalidPostcodeException` et al. `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler` is structurally identical to `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler`, emitting the same `{\"errors\": [\"<field>\"]}` 400 shape the codebase uses for single-field validation failures. This is the chosen per-field convention here (dedicated exception/handler per field), so reproducing that two-line body is consistency, not stray duplication.\n- **Wiring is natural.** The new `validateRegistrationDate` step is slotted among the other front-loaded field validations (after `ValidateNewOwner`, before `validatePostcode`), keeping all \"reject before building/persisting\" 400 checks together, with a comment matching the surrounding style. The escalation YAML follows the `<fully.qualified.Exception>.yml` convention.\n\nNo logic is misplaced, nothing is becoming a catch-all, and a maintainer of this codebase would find it indistinguishable from the existing validation steps.",
